WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.320
With fertile pastoral plains, productive
horticultural sectors,

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:07.800
rugged high country
and ecologically significant estuaries.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:09.600
The Waimakariri District's

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:13.160
economy and character is dependent
upon the natural environment.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:16.000
So as we head into the 21st century,

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:19.480
it is critical to understand
how environment is changing.

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:24.240
So we can prepare, find opportunities,
adapt and continue to prosper.

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:28.080
Climate change is already happening
in the Waimakariri

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:31.760
and the district is about one degree
warmer than 100 years ago.

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:35.160
This recent warming
has been caused by an increase

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:38.000
in greenhouse
gas concentrations in our atmosphere.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:42.080
These extra gas molecules
cause more of the sun's radiation

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:46.520
and heat to be pushed back down to earth
rather than escaping into space.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:51.000
Future changes to temperature,
rainfall patterns as well as sea level

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.320
rise will have impacts
on our communities, businesses

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:57.440
and natural environments.

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:01.320
When talking about
potential climate futures in this video,

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:05.480
we use two different scenarios
of greenhouse gas concentrations.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:08.920
One is a high range scenario,
with greenhouse gas

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:11.520
concentrations continue to rise unabated.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:17.000
The other is a moderate scenario where
concentrations stabilize around 2060.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:18.720
Given

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:21.720
we do not know which
scenario will eventuate, it's important

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:25.880
to examine the potential changes
and impacts associated with both scenarios

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:29.040
so we can fully understand
and explore the risks.

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:33.600
Greenhouse gases can stay
in the atmosphere for a long time,

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:37.440
so we're still likely to see
continued climate change into the future

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:44.800
despite efforts to reduce emissions.

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:49.480
Temperature increases could impact water
supplies, encourage the spread of pests

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:52.640
and place pressure
on key growth stages of some crops.

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:57.000
However, increased temperatures
may also allow new crops to be grown

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:00.080
in the district, and warmer
air temperatures could have other benefits

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:03.640
for agriculture, such as reduce stress
experienced by livestock

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:06.120
during winter.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:09.560
Increasing temperatures
in the Waimakariri District

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:12.120
mean that extreme heat
will be more common.

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:17.000
The District currently experiences
up to 40 hot days per year.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:21.920
This is projected to increase by up
to 20 days by the middle of the century

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:23.320
under the moderate scenario.

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:27.840
For the high range
scenario, the average number of hot days

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:30.240
in the district
could triple by the end of the century.

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:34.120
There is a large variation
in the number of hot days

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:39.240
across the region with more in the low
elevation areas than in the mountains.

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:43.280
The Lees Valley and Western Plains
could see upwards of 60

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:47.400
additional hot days per year
if future emissions are unrestricted.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:52.160
The increase in hot days
is likely to place increased heat

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:55.800
stress on livestock, contribute
to a loss of biodiversity,

00:02:56.120 --> 00:03:00.000
impact the health of freshwater
ecosystems, and lead to increased

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.120
energy used for cooling.

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:08.160
Minimum temperatures
are also projected to warm,

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:12.160
which will result in fewer frost
and snow days for the Waimakariri.

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:17.280
Frosts are most common for inland and high
elevation areas of the District.

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:21.360
But by the end of the century up to 30
fewer frosts are projected

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:25.360
under the mid range scenario
with up to 50 fewer frosts under the high

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:34.040
range scenario.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:37.440
Projected changes to total annual rainfall

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:41.640
a varied in the Waimakariri District,
but are generally expected to increase.

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:46.280
This occurs in part because as the planet
warms, the atmosphere can hold

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:50.280
more moisture, leading to more
total annual rainfall in the District.

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:53.240
The eastern and southern plains
will see the largest

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:57.160
increases in rainfall,
with about 8 to 12% more rain annually.

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:01.160
While slight decrease is projected
in the western high elevations,

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:04.520
this east to west pattern
and the precipitation and changes

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:08.440
is fairly consistent across the seasons
except in spring,

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:13.560
which may experience a larger decrease
in rainfall than the other seasons.

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:17.680
Residents of the District can also expect
more frequent and severe extreme rainfall

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:21.600
events and storms as the climate warms
during the 21st century.

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:25.560
Such storms could lead to flooding
in the district's large river systems,

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:30.040
disrupt agriculture, damage
transport networks and private property.

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:34.440
Despite a projected increase
in total rainfall for the district,

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:38.240
increased evapotranspiration
resulting from warmer temperatures

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:41.320
means that drought is also expected
to be more common

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:44.040
and more severe in the Waimakariri.

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:47.040
This may impact access
to reliable water supplies

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:51.160
for towns, agriculture, horticulture
and the natural environment.

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:55.640
A drier and warmer
environment is also likely to experience

00:04:55.640 --> 00:04:58.640
more wildfire.

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:03.320
Coastal land
and settlements in the District

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:07.200
could be directly impacted
by sea level rise and coastal inundation.

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:11.840
This will likely increase coastal erosion
and flooding, which could damage homes

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:14.400
and infrastructure
such as pipes and roads.

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:17.720
The NIWA report contains projections

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:20.960
for many more climatic variables,
which you can see here.

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:24.400
These climate projections show
we may face significant

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:27.560
challenges heading into the future,

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:31.400
reducing our emissions is urgently
needed to stabilize the climate.

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:36.480
But it is also vital that we identify
adaptation strategies like strengthening

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:41.520
the reliability of our water supplies,
improving biodiversity and river quality.

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:46.200
Climate change will affect all of us,
and the Council will need to work

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:50.680
in partnership with Ngāi Tūāhuriri Rūnanga
as kaitiaki of the Rohe.

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:55.280
We hope open communication of the science
projections will both motivate

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:58.960
and inform discussions with businesses
and the wider community.

00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:03.760
The goal is to have all our community
working together to build resilience

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:05.240
and adapt to climate change.

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:08.000
Climate change is

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:11.120
a challenge that everyone needs to address
from government through to individuals.

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:12.320
We all play a part.

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:16.000
That's why Waimakariri has got a policy
in place and a program of works to ensure

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:16.920
that we do everything

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:20.480
we can to futureproof our communities
against the challenges that lay ahead.

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:23.240
The information in this
NIWA report is critical

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:25.800
to understanding the scale of the changes
that are in front of us.

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:29.240
Delivering a climate resilient
future needs all regions

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:33.400
to take action to reduce transport
emissions, improve building productivity,

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:38.040
maximize energy efficiency, lessen waste
and change consumption habits.

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:40.800
We're aiming to improve the Council's
responsiveness,

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:42.880
provide transformational leadership,

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:45.720
and take a planned approach
to mitigating and reducing emissions.

00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:49.920
We're also relying on you as we work
collaboratively with the community

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:52.840
and other stakeholders to adaptively plan
for climate change effects

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:54.600
and increase our resilience.

00:06:54.600 --> 00:07:08.800
It's critical
for our District and its wellbeing.

